// ==== src/paint/brush.js
// paint/brush.js : the loaded brush on the paint layer (plan section 2.2, lane P1).  Nothing is drawn.  A brush is N bristles, each with
// its own across offset, tuft length and paint load; at every sample of the arm's gesture each bristle tests its contact against the
// surface under it, picks up the movable paint it drags through, and deposits what its load and the contact allow.  Scumble, dry brush,
// ragged edges, square or feathered ends, translucency, the raised edge of a fat wet stroke and the greying of a white dragged through
// wet grey all happen in the layer; nothing draws them on top.
//
//   PK.paint.brushStroke(L, rng, path, opts) -> 1 | 0   one stroke.  L = PK.kit.paint (null -> PK.kit.paint).  path in USER px.
//   PK.paint.brushFill(L, rng, poly, opts)   -> strokes  a shape filled with long strokes along its axis: the stamp brushFill's lane logic
//                                                        (bands at (1 - overlap) w, edge bands inset so their edge sits on the polygon's edge,
//                                                        under coat first, cross pass, loaded and cover quotas, contour), every band a physical
//                                                        stroke along the shape's FORM (FORM 1): its medial line along the fill's axis and its
//                                                        width across, sampled at 28 stations and smoothed; a band is a curve at a fixed
//                                                        fraction of the local half width, so a bent shape is filled with bent strokes and a
//                                                        crescent's bands follow its arc (G23).  FORM 0: straight chords (the planted negative).
//                                                        brushStroke into the wet paint of the bands before it.
//   PK.paint.dab(L, rng, path, opts)          -> dabs     one loaded touch per point: a round brush pressed, moved a little and lifted (heavy
//                                                        centre, thin rim, a dry side where it left), through the same bristles.
//   PK.paint.brush.K                                     the constants (the planted controls zero DRAIN, PICK, TOOTH_K and RIDGE).
//   Log: PK.gesture('brushStroke' | 'dab', path, opts) and PK.container('brushFill', ...) exactly as the stamp brush, so lint and replay read
//   the same marks; L.tick() once per gesture.  One draw from the caller's rng per stroke (a fork); the arm's gesture takes one more.
//
// THE MODEL (per stroke).
//   bristles   N = clamp(round(w sx / SPACING), 12, 96), one tip per device px across at nominal pressure; across offsets u in -1..1 (even,
//              jittered), load 0.95..1 x load0 each, and a wander: the whole tuft sways across the stroke (0.3..0.7 px, wavelength 40..110 px)
//              and each hair drifts a little of its own (0.04..0.15 px; the outer hairs 0.3..0.7 more), so no two lay a rule.
//   BUNDLES    (round 4, P1e.)  The hairs are bunched into bundles of BUNDLE_MIN..BUNDLE_MAX consecutive hairs (2 to 4 device px; a 40 px flat
//              carries about 13).  A bundle shares its along offset (a square end is a few flat steps), its stop stagger (GROUP), its tuft
//              length (0.9..1, each hair within 2% of it) and its REACH r in 0..1 (0 the longest bundle, 1 the shortest; each hair jittered by
//              BUNDLE_JR).  At every sample the tuft's compression is cmp = pressure / BUNDLE_P x (BUNDLE_B + (1 - BUNDLE_B) body): a bundle
//              with r beyond cmp is lifted by BUNDLE_H x clamp((r - cmp) / BUNDLE_W) contact units and its hairs lay nothing; below it the
//              bundle is down.  A pressed full brush (0.7, body 1: cmp 1.27) is one blade, as before; a thin drag (body 0.4: cmp 0.62) or a
//              light pass (press 0.35: 0.64) has a third of its bundles off the surface, and what it lays is bundles of FULL colour 2 to 4 px
//              wide with ground between (paint_brush_bundles).  The hairs of a bundle share a contact state: each hair's target is BUNDLE_J
//              of its own (its own reach jitter, the weave under it) and the rest the bundle's mean, so a bundle touches and lifts together
//              and the weave's valleys lift bundles, not single hairs.  Nava's thin drags are hair bundles, not hairs (P1d).
//   footprint  w (FOOT0 + FOOT1 pressure) (1 - taperEnd t): w at the nava hand's 0.7 pressure; edge pressure p (1 - EDGE_P u^2).
//   contact    want = base + (TOOTH_K devT + HGT_K devH) (1 - base)^2, clamped 0..1, base = PRESS_K p tuft (LOAD_C + (1 - LOAD_C) min(1, load)):
//              a pressed loaded tuft flattens into the weave and the surface terms vanish; a light or empty one feels them.  devT = the weave
//              showing under the hair (surfaceAt - hgt) minus its mean under the footprint, in rms units of the bare weave, capped at DEV_CAP;
//              devH = the paint height minus its mean under the footprint, in paint units, capped at HGT_CAP (hairs bend over a ridge of old
//              paint; they do not skip the whole low side).  Each hair's state follows want with a lag (LAG per px) and lays nothing below
//              DEAD: a dry brush leaves streaks along the stroke where the weave keeps a hair down, not a dot per tooth cell.  A pressed
//              loaded brush is in full contact (0.95 at nominal); a light or nearly empty one rides the peaks: scumble and dry brush.  Over
//              paint the weave is filled (layer.js toothVisible) and the same brush lays a smooth coat.  The LOAD's share of the contact
//              decides only WHERE a hair lays (a nearly empty hair rides the peaks), never how thick: the film is the pressure's (touch =
//              the hair's state at full load, then the dead zone), so a loaded stroke running out lays its full film to the last hair, and
//              a light brush lays thin only where it barely touches.  (The scumble this makes is a light brush laying full-colour marks
//              where the weave brings a hair down, not round two's thin veil over the peaks; see G5's control for the load it now wants.)
//   tuft       paint flows within the tuft: every hair's load relaxes toward the tuft's mean with e-fold 1 / FLOW widths (the pressed hairs
//              draw paint from the lifted ones), and a hair RELEASES by its share (lf, the HAIR_LOAD stagger) of the tuft's MEAN load, not
//              its own (MEANREL): the mean only falls, so each hair lays its full film until its share of the tuft is at REL_END, then stops
//              for good.  Judged on its own load with the flow topping it up as fast as it spent, a hair hovered at its stopping point laying
//              half a film for 100 px: the pale veil by another road (measured, P1c: the last third's covered cells at 0.6 of the body's
//              height and colour distance 0.18; with MEANREL 0.87 and 0.046).
//   sampling   the arm's samples are a px apart; a sample is sub-stepped (up to 4) when the tangent turns, since the outer hairs of a fat
//              brush sweep hf x the turn further than the centre (the outer wheel in a bend) and would skip cells.  Tangent over +-4 samples.
//   pickup     where a cell's movable paint is from an EARLIER stroke, take PICK x contact of it into the hair's load (colour-weighted):
//              the load greys through wet grey and not through dry.  A cell this stroke has already touched is not picked up again.
//   deposit    rate = DEP x touch x (1 - 0.5 speed) x release(load) x body x alpha per device px of travel, laid where the hair is (a tent
//              over the four cells around its continuous position, TRACK).  release = 1 while the hair's share of the tuft's load is over
//              REL_LOW and 0 at REL_END (REL_LOW = REL_END: no knee; a knee, however short in load, is long in px at the dry end, where the
//              mean drains at the rate of the few hairs still laying, so the last hairs smeared half films for 10 to 50 px; REL_P shapes a
//              knee if one is set).  A hair gives its full film, thick and opaque at the stroke's colour, to its last px and then STOPS (the
//              remainder REL_END never comes off).  Hairs stop one by one (HAIR_LOAD staggers the share each hair can spend, GROUP how much of
//              that stagger is its hair group's), so a dry-out is full-colour streaks with ground between them, thinning in COUNT over the
