//              brush's width; so a w 8 brush at load 3 runs 40 to 60 w and a w 50 brush 8 to 10 w (a thin brush holds many widths, as in
//              life).  VOL 0 is round two's rule, load spent per width travelled (DRAIN_W / w), G21's planted negative.
//   DRAG       (round 5, P5b step 2.)  A hair whose film has run out does not stop dead.  Dragged on, it rubs the paint in the heel of the
//              tuft (DRAG_RES per hair, x min(1, load0), x the hair's share lf / 0.7) onto what it still touches: a full-depth film where the
//              hair is down, nothing where its bundle is lifted (the tuft's binding is gone under BUNDLE_DRY, so the bundles stand apart and
//              clumped) or the weave drops away under it (the low load has taken it off the low cells, lq).  The heel is spent as the film
//              was (DRAIN x what is laid), hairs down spend and lifted ones do not, and the hairs with the larger share hold more, so the
//              marks thin by COUNT over a width or two past the film's end, one bundle at a time, never by depth (paint_brush_dragout: the
//              last third of a stroke that runs out at half its length covers 0.3 to 0.6 in runs of bundles with 1 to 3 px of ground between;
//              DRAG 0 is the law before round 5, a dry end one width long at a line; DRAG_VEIL 1 the thinning even film).  A dry hair picks
//              up what is wet under it into its empty tip (the wipe: black at no load through wet black comes up grey); it piles no ridge.
//   levelling  paint laid into a cell still wet from an EARLIER stroke does not stack in full: LEVEL of min(wet height there, new height)
//              is pushed aside (the brush pushes the wet film).  The bands of a fill meet at about 1.3 of a band's height, not 2.
